# The bundle: the conventions that ship as RULES rather than as branches (§4).
#
# There is one, and that is a finding rather than an oversight. This file held
# nineteen rules: what an emission means, what a taken act means, the agent
# asserting its own acts, denial as a term reconciled with denial as a sign,
# four ways an expectation can be disappointed, means-ends expansion, asking
# whether a subgoal is already met, giving up, and a three-rule call stack.
#
# Every one of them was a policy about how to conduct oneself -- when to act,
# when to stop, how to decompose a task, what to do when the world surprises
# you -- and none of them was a convention of READING. They went with the
# machinery they were written against, and an agent that wants any of them back
# writes it into its own corpus, where a claim about how to conduct oneself
# belongs.
#
# What is left is the one that really is about reading. It also happens to be
# the one that could not be written by anyone but the engine until this file
# existed: `arrived` was unnameable in a corpus, so <intake> was unwritable,
# and nothing anywhere reported that.


# -- the boundary ----------------------------------------------------------

# What a report MEANS. Crossing the boundary stays machinery, because a channel
# is anchored and a rule is generic; deciding that an arrival is a saying does
# not, and never did.
#
# The sign went with the entry. An arrival used to carry one -- `arrived($ch,
# $said, $sign)` -- and under the scratchpad a channel reports what it heard,
# not what to do about it. A channel that wants to report a denial reports
# `not($p)`, which is an ordinary term and says the same thing without needing
# a third argument nobody could argue with.
#  The guard is the corpus's, not the engine's. There is no inert set: an
# application that changed nothing is offered again, so a rule that does not
# stop itself never stops. `no says(...)` is the whole of the stopping, and it
# is a premise -- readable, overridable, and wrong out loud if it is wrong.
rule <intake> = implies(
    { +arrived($channel, $said), no says($channel, $said) },
    { +says($channel, $said) } )
